{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310540,"nonce":"Zln+MdLamERoptqbIUwQAVAuAG8=","title":"FIXED 013aad05 — infer_expression_type had NO arm for \u0026 | ^ \u003c\u003c \u003e\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e, so bitwise results typed Dynamic; a later `if (c) v = K` boxed the ...","message":"Found 2026-08-02 validating the Haxe Q4_K→f16 dequant. **FIXED `013aad05`.**\n\n`ast_lowering.rs::infer_expression_type` had arms for arithmetic, comparison\nand assignment operators but **none for `\u0026 | ^ \u003c\u003c \u003e\u003e \u003e\u003e\u003e`** — they fell to the\n`_ =\u003e Ok(dynamic_type())` catch-all. So:\n\n```haxe\nvar r = x \u003e\u003e 13;           // r : Dynamic   \u003c-- root cause\nr++;\nif (r == 0x400) { r = 0; } // boxes K via haxe_box_int_ptr; phi degrades to *void\nreturn r;                  // returns the BOX POINTER truncated to i32\n```\n\nMIR proof:\n```\nbb1: $7 = const 0i32 ; $8 = call fn430119($7)   ; = haxe_box_int_ptr\nbb3: $11 = phi *void [bb1: $8], [bb2: $4]\n     $12 = bitcast $11 to i64 ; $13 = cast i64 $12 to i32 ; ret $13\n```\nWrong value **and** 48 bytes leaked per taken branch. `+ - * % ~` were already\ncorrect; only the six bitwise/shift ops were affected.\n\n**Fix**: a real arm returning Int, with user Class/Abstract operands winning\n(so `@:op` overloads like masking a `Usize` keep their type) and\nDynamic-in→Dynamic-out matching the arithmetic arm's convention.\n\nTWO conditions must coincide: the local comes from a bitwise expr AND is\nconditionally reassigned. A single reassign whose branch is **never taken**\ncompiles the same bad code but never runs it — `var r = mant \u003e\u003e 13; if (r ==\n0x400) r = 0;` is unreachable for a 23-bit mantissa, so my first ladder rung\nscored a FALSE PASS and I nearly concluded \"a prior reassignment is required\".\n**Check a branch is reachable before reading its result as evidence.**\n\n- [[bugs_int8_model_footprint_climb]] — the SpinPool `if (c) v = \u003cconst\u003e`\n  boxing (~1 GB/request) was worked around in nue with a ternary. THIS was the\n  real cause; the compiler is now fixed.\n- The \"in-loop running `if (d\u003emax) max=d` returns a value outside the f16 bit\n  range\" trap recorded in [[perf_llamacpp_2026_08_mistral_prefill_gap]] was\n  **not** a separate conditional-int-reassign codegen defect — the *inputs*\n  were pointers from this bug. Running max/min in a loop is fine.\n- Ternary (`v = c ? K : v`) worked because it never takes the assignment\n  coercion path — that is why it kept being an effective workaround.\n\nThe Haxe `dequantQ4KToF16` vs the plugin was **8027/16777216 = 0.048%**\ndiffering, which I attributed to f32-FMA vs f64 rounding. That was wrong: it\nwas this bug. Post-fix the two are **BIT-IDENTICAL over all 16.7M elements** of\na real 4096×4096 Mistral Q4_K_M weight, serial deterministic, pooled == serial.\nNo token-level A/B was needed. See [[project_pure_haxe_tensor_ownership]].\n\n`RAYZOR_ASSIGN_DEBUG=1` prints `[ASSIGN] \u003cname\u003e target=\u003ckind\u003e rhs=\u003ckind\u003e` at\neach assignment coercion. **A primitive local showing `target=Dynamic` is the\nshape that boxes.** This named the bug in one run after the runtime bisect\nstalled.\n\n- `rayzor dump -O 0 --function f` for MIR; the `; fnN = @name` legend is valid\n  ONLY within the same dump — two runs gave `fn430123` then `fn430119` for the\n  same callee. Always re-resolve in the dump you are reading.\n- `StringTools.hex(v, 4)` printed **pointers** for these values, sending me\n  chasing a phantom. Print decimal when a value's provenance is in doubt.\n- Regression test: `compiler/tests/haxe/test_bitwise_conditional_reassign.hx`.\n  Suite 168/168.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_bitwise_infer_dynamic_boxes_reassign` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}